d of one week or two weeks  the curve goes up again why because the  vaccine comes you know through the  exchanges that we cannot disrupt no  country can lock itself away from the  rest of the world and therefore  it is imperative that the vaccine issue  remains within the realm of the  of the health issue and politics cannot  and should not become a factor  um but the fact is that we do see china  for instance  uh taking a much more uh active role in  the middle east  you yourself co-hosted a forum with wang  yi just last year  to talk about greater chinese in  engagement in the middle east  some observers look at a kind of a big  trend with the united states pulling  back in the middle east and china coming  in is that something you observe  well um the us has a leading role in in  the middle east and and that role cannot  really be  replaced at this point europe has a  leading role in the middle east the  middle east has been  has been unfortunately a an area where  conflicts have continued to grow and  expand uh unless we work together as  partners we're not going to be able to  resolve those issues  china does have a strong economic ties  with the region  not much with jordan but regionally  speaking  there is a a lot of economic cooperation  trade cooperation purchase of oil and  all of that so  uh that is a reality but as far as  jordan is concerned we  always see good relations with everybody  based on  our you know international law on our  values where we want cooperation rather  than conflict where  we want relations to be transparent and  based on  on grounds that allow for a mutual  benefit for all  i'd like to return to the question of  refugees you know  jordan is hosting many hundreds of  thousands of syrian refugees  um at the moment you said today that it  it's not yet possible for them to return  um but have you been having discussions  with the syrian government about  the possibility at some point of  returning  look again we have 1.3 million sy